{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1511 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "4dc0e39f67989d35b4d36d374a420dd2523e6d36176f7b91473a22bc470ca66e" :edb/db-before-t 1510 :edb/db-after-t 1511 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534823 50 #inst "2026-10-08T08:08:38.502Z" 13194139534823 true] [17592186064197 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312545 13194139534823 true] [17592186064197 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312566 13194139534823 true] [17592186064225 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312538 13194139534823 true] [17592186064225 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312552 13194139534823 true] [17592186064225 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312559 13194139534823 true] [17592186312538 1017 #edb/ref 17592186312539 13194139534823 true] [17592186312538 1089 "q-113899" 13194139534823 true] [17592186312538 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534823 true] [17592186312538 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/42fd32f22d25ed1d26d48ca8d4f1f5f454bcca67c68febaec066fa3b8bde0783.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave down *and* ${f}^{′}(x)<0?$" 13194139534823 true] [17592186312538 1113 #uuid "4fd2bf14-4068-54c2-b1dc-152d783a9acf" 13194139534823 true] [17592186312538 1145 "We begin by noticing that at $x=a$ the graph has a corner point, which implies that the derivative doesn't exist there.\n\nThe derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is negative when $f(x)$ is decreasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=c$ are on decreasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/f6e6c7b869268eba113690776b0aa07713ff12adb675e22e11406c4a13f0e7b2.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mn